// Practice quiz: ArrayList basics
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so generic types are written as List of Integer.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/arrays-lists-and-maps/arraylist-basics

::o1 set versus add::A List of String named tasks holds "wash", "cook". Then tasks.set(0, "shop"); runs. What does tasks hold? {
=[shop, cook]#Correct. set replaces the element at that index, and the size stays 2.
~[shop, wash, cook]#Not quite. That would be add(0, "shop"), which inserts and shifts.
~[wash, shop]#Not quite. Index 0 is the first element, not the second.
~[wash, cook, shop]#Not quite. set never adds to the end; it replaces.
}

::o1 size after changes::A new ArrayList of String gets add("a"), add("b"), add("c"), then remove(0). What does size() return? {
=2#Correct. Three adds make 3 elements, and one remove leaves 2.
~3#Not quite. remove(0) takes an element out, so the size drops.
~1#Not quite. Only one element was removed.
~It throws, because index 0 was removed#Not quite. The others shift left, and size() never throws.
}

::o2 growing count::Which of these is the best fit for an ArrayList rather than an array? {
=The orders placed in a shop today, as they arrive#Correct. The count grows during the day, and a list grows with it.
~The 26 letters of the English alphabet#Not quite. The count is fixed, so a plain array fits.
~The 12 months of a year#Not quite. The count never changes, so a plain array fits.
~The 64 squares of a chess board#Not quite. The board size is fixed, so a plain array fits.
}

::o2 why switch::Code stores names in String[] names \= new String[5]; and a sixth name arrives. What is the real problem? {
=An array cannot grow, so there is no room for a sixth element#Correct. An ArrayList grows as elements are added.
~Arrays cannot hold String values#Not quite. Arrays hold any type, including String.
~names[5] already holds a name#Not quite. Index 5 does not exist at all in a length-5 array.
~Arrays are slower to read than lists#Not quite. Reading is not the issue; the fixed size is.
}

::o3 primitive type argument::Why does javac reject List of int as a type? {
=Type arguments may be reference types or wildcards; here Integer is a reference type and int is primitive#Correct. Java then boxes each int into an Integer for you.
~Lists can only hold String values#Not quite. A list can hold reference types such as String, Integer or Double.
~int is too small to store in a list#Not quite. Size has nothing to do with it; int is a primitive, not a class.
~List must be written as ArrayList#Not quite. List of Integer is fine; the problem is the primitive int.
}

::o3 wrapper for decimals::You need a list of decimal prices such as 2.5 and 3.75. Which element type works? {
=Double#Correct. Double is the wrapper class for the primitive double.
~double#Not quite. A primitive cannot be a generic type argument.
~Integer#Not quite. Integer holds whole numbers only, so 2.5 would not fit.
~Decimal#Not quite. Java has no Decimal class; the wrapper is Double.
}
